# Node-27 published-frontier stall alerting (issue #1368 / OpenSpec:
# node27-frontier-stall-alert).
#
# Copy to node27-frontier-alert.env and chmod 0600 — the wrapper
# (scripts/node27_frontier_stall_alert_once.sh) refuses a missing, symlinked
# or non-0600 env file.
#
# This lane only READS the database and only sends mail through the local
# sendmail binary. It never writes to Postgres and holds no SMTP credentials.
#
# SINGLE READER: under systemd this file is read once by
# `EnvironmentFile=` in nhms-node27-frontier-alert.service. The wrapper only
# sources it when DATABASE_URL is NOT already injected (i.e. manual debugging
# from a shell), so no value is ever parsed by both grammars in one run.
# Values here must nevertheless mean the same thing to BOTH parsers: quote any
# value containing spaces (systemd strips the surrounding quotes, so does the
# shell). WARNING: a password containing  $ ` " \  is NOT dual-grammar safe —
# bash expands/eats those inside double quotes while systemd does not. If the
# read-only role's password needs such characters, use single quotes and verify
# both paths, or (preferred) pick a password from [A-Za-z0-9._~-].

NODE27_FRONTIER_ALERT_REPO=/home/nwm/NWM

# Node-27 primary Postgres — use the READ-ONLY role DSN (nhms_display_ro).
# The alerter runs one read-only aggregate over hydro.hydro_run; a writer role
# here would hand a monitoring lane needless mutation rights (least privilege).
# The runner supplies its own bounded connect_timeout (10 s) and
# statement_timeout (30 s); do NOT append connect_timeout=0 or any unbounded
# libpq keyword here — an unbounded connect would let the watchdog hang the
# same way the thing it watches did.
DATABASE_URL=postgresql://nhms_display_ro:REPLACE_ME_PW@127.0.0.1:55432/nhms

# Alert recipient. REQUIRED, no default: an unset value fails the run closed
# (structured config error, exit 2) rather than silently mailing nobody.
# A CR/LF anywhere in this value is rejected at config time (header injection).
NHMS_ALERT_EMAIL_TO=mumzy1995@163.com

# Optional envelope sender. Default: "NHMS Frontier Alert <nwm@$(hostname)>".
# Quoted because the value contains spaces: systemd strips the surrounding
# double quotes and so does the shell, so both readers see the same string.
#NHMS_ALERT_EMAIL_FROM="NHMS Frontier Alert <nwm@node-27>"

# Stall window in hours. Default 4. Rationale: a full measured pass is
# 2h16m-3h01m plus 15-30 min ingest lag, so the realistic upper bound of a
# healthy production cycle is ~3h30m; 4 h leaves margin without hiding a real
# outage for long. Progress is directional (new source, or a strict increase
# of frontier / distinct-cycle count / latest arrival) and is NEVER compared
# against wall-clock lag — while backfilling, the frontier is legitimately
# days behind.
NHMS_FRONTIER_STALL_HOURS=4

# Resend window in hours while a stall persists. Default 6 (one stall = one
# mail, then at most one mail per 6 h until progress resumes).
NHMS_FRONTIER_RESEND_HOURS=6

# Absolute path — persisted high-water baseline + alert state (mode 0600,
# atomic tmp+rename). The single-instance lockfile is derived from it
# (<state path>.lock) and the whole directory belongs to this lane.
# Deleting this file is indistinguishable from a first install: the next tick
# bootstraps a fresh baseline silently (blind window <= NHMS_FRONTIER_STALL_HOURS).
NHMS_FRONTIER_STATE_PATH=/home/nwm/node27-frontier-alert-logs/frontier-alert-state.json

# Absolute path — latest-tick observation receipt (atomically overwritten).
# The append-only alert event log is derived beside it as
# <receipt dir>/frontier-alert-events.jsonl.
NHMS_FRONTIER_RECEIPT_PATH=/home/nwm/node27-frontier-alert-logs/frontier-alert-receipt.json

# Absolute path to the local sendmail binary (invoked as `sendmail -t -i`).
NHMS_FRONTIER_SENDMAIL=/usr/sbin/sendmail

# Consecutive failed observation ticks before an observability-unavailable
# alert is raised. Default 2 (= 1 h at the 30 min timer cadence). Query
# failures never reset the stall clock.
NHMS_FRONTIER_QUERY_FAIL_TICKS=2
